# Cluster magnetic octupole induced out-of-plane spin polarization in antiperovskite antiferromagnet

_Nougat-style interim extraction (equations + core claims). Source: plaintext pdftotext of You et al. 2021._

## Abstract (verbatim excerpt)
Out-of-plane spin polarization \(\sigma_z\) ... Noncollinear antiferromagnet (AFM) ... has been discovered to possess \(\sigma_z\). Here we use the cluster magnetic octupole in antiperovskite AFM Mn\(_3\)SnN to demonstrate the generation of \(\sigma_z\). ... With the aid of \(\sigma_z\), current induced spin-orbit torque (SOT) switching of adjacent perpendicular ferromagnet is realized without external magnetic field.

## Governing relations
Generation of out-of-plane spin polarization:
\[ \boldsymbol{\sigma}_z \;\propto\; \mathbf{H}_{so} \times \mathbf{T} \tag{1} \]
with \(\mathbf{T}\) the cluster magnetic octupole moment and \(\mathbf{H}_{so}\perp\mathbf{J}\).

Symmetry rule: \(\sigma_z\) present when \(\mathbf{J}\parallel\mathbf{T}\); \(\sigma_z=0\) when \(\mathbf{J}\perp\mathbf{T}\) (broken magnetic mirror symmetry \(M'=M\!\cdot\!T\)).

SOT torque terms on FM magnetization \(\mathbf{m}\):
\[ \tau_S \propto \mathbf{m}\times(\mathbf{m}\times\boldsymbol{\sigma}_y), \quad
   \tau_B \propto \mathbf{m}\times\boldsymbol{\sigma}_z, \quad
   \tau_C \propto \mathbf{m}\times(\mathbf{m}\times\boldsymbol{\sigma}_z). \]

ST-FMR line-shape:
\[ V_{mix}(H) = V_S \frac{\Delta H^2}{\Delta H^2+(H-H_0)^2} + V_A \frac{\Delta H\,(H-H_0)}{\Delta H^2+(H-H_0)^2} \tag{2} \]

Kittel: \( f = \frac{\gamma}{2\pi}\left[H_0(H_0+M_{eff})\right]^{1/2} \).

## Quantitative results
\(\theta_{AD,z}=0.003\pm0.001\), \(\theta_{FL,z}=0.053\pm0.005\); field-free \(J_{crit}\approx 9\times10^{6}\,\mathrm{A\,cm^{-2}}\); \(T_N=475\,\mathrm{K}\).

## Central claim reproduced here
The \(\tau_C=\mathbf{m}\times(\mathbf{m}\times\boldsymbol{\sigma}_z)\) antidamping term lifts the \(\pm z\) degeneracy of a PMA magnet with NO external field, giving deterministic switching whose final state is fixed by the sign of \(J\). Absent \(\sigma_z\) (\(\mathbf{J}\perp\mathbf{T}\)), only \(\sigma_y\) remains and no deterministic field-free switching occurs.
